{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a prominent figure in the Crusades and a ruler of the County of Tripoli during the 12th century. He was the son of Raymond I Count of Tripoli and a key player in the political and military landscape of the Levant. His reign marked a period of significant expansion and consolidation of Tripoli's power and influence.\n\n*Early Life and Rise to Power* Raymond III inherited the County of Tripoli in 1183 upon the death of his father. He was a skilled military leader and administrator quickly establishing himself as a strong ruler. His early reign was characterized by successful military campaigns against Muslim forces in Syria and Palestine. He focused on strengthening Tripoli's defenses and expanding its territory. Raymond III actively participated in the Third Crusade leading Tripoli forces in several battles. His military prowess and strategic thinking earned him considerable respect among his peers and his soldiers. He also demonstrated diplomatic skills forging alliances with various powers including the Holy Roman Empire and the Papacy.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was also marked by significant economic development. He fostered trade relations with European merchants promoting the growth of Tripoli as a major commercial center in the Eastern Mediterranean. He invested in infrastructure projects improving roads and ports to facilitate trade and communication. Tripoli under Raymond III became a vital link between Europe and the East a hub for goods and ideas. He promoted a diverse population attracting merchants artisans and scholars from various parts of the world.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts* Raymond III’s military career was extensive and impactful. He led numerous campaigns against the Ayyubid Sultanate of Egypt and Syria. These campaigns were often costly and involved significant casualties but they were crucial in maintaining Tripoli's independence and preventing its conquest. He also engaged in conflicts with other Crusader states and Muslim factions in the region. His military successes solidified Tripoli's position as a major power in the Levant. He was known for his tactical acumen and his ability to adapt to changing circumstances in battle.\n\n*Legacy and Later Life* Raymond III died in 1203 while leading a campaign against the Ayyubids. His death marked the end of a successful era in Tripoli's history. He is remembered as a capable and courageous ruler who defended the county's independence and prosperity. He left behind a legacy of military strength economic growth and cultural diversity. His reign set the stage for the future development of the County of Tripoli as a significant power in the Crusader states. His contributions to the region's political and economic landscape continue to be studied and appreciated by historians.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.1804991215467453}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -6.115249561844394e-05}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁was", "logprob": -0.08474750816822052}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.031199738383293152}, {"token": "▁prominent", "logprob": -1.382148027420044}, {"token": "▁figure", "logprob": -0.022309403866529465}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.005190112628042698}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0239579901099205}, {"token": "▁Crus", "logprob": -0.9676498174667358}, {"token": "ades", "logprob": -0.49816298484802246}, {"token": "▁and", "logprob": -0.5369524359703064}, {"token": "▁a", "logprob": -0.09521526098251343}, {"token": "▁ruler", "logprob": -0.45563167333602905}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -0.052740734070539474}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0034867464564740658}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.03704468905925751}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -2.2291887944447808